# Plan 4 Task 6, D86 (the ruling on java.net): a box's program talks to nobody, so java.net
# fails as it does on a computer with no network, with an exception the program can catch. TeaVM's
# http(s) URLConnection (TXHRURLConnection) sent its request with an asynchronous XMLHttpRequest, which a
# program in this runtime can never finish: with the page's network doors closed (D86) the offline stub's
# send() threw, and the reader saw TeaVM's wrapper of that JavaScript error
# (org.teavm.jso.impl.wasmgc.WasmGCExceptionWrapper), a RuntimeException no catch of IOException sees; in
# Node, which has no XMLHttpRequest, the same wrapper said "XMLHttpRequest is not defined". Now the
# connection makes no request at all:
#   - connect(), getInputStream() and getOutputStream() throw, and getResponseCode() and
#     getResponseMessage() throw through connect(), as HttpURLConnection does when it cannot connect;
#     the header getters fall back to the base classes, which answer as for a failed connection.
#   - A host name throws java.net.UnknownHostException with the host as its message: what the pinned
#     JDK prints for a .invalid host, which never resolves (RFC 6761), and so what it prints offline
#     (runtime/test/cases.mjs, the D86 cases, pin it against the JDK).
#   - An address literal (four numbers 0 to 255 joined by dots, or an IPv6 address in brackets) needs no
#     lookup, so it throws java.net.ConnectException("Network is unreachable"), the operating system's
#     words for ENETUNREACH. That one is not pinned against the JDK: its machine has a network, so the
#     JDK's offline answer for an address cannot be observed there.
#   - Loopback is teavm-0020's: on top of this patch, 127.0.0.1 and localhost fail as the JDK does when
#     nothing listens there (D100).
#   - UnknownHostException, ConnectException and SocketException (ConnectException's superclass in the
#     JDK) are new to TeaVM's class library. Like every class there, teavm-javac's StdlibConverter puts
#     them into the compile classlib under their JDK names, so javac sees java.net.UnknownHostException;
#     teavm-javac-0102 shows the other way, a class kept out of that classlib.
# The XMLHttpRequest code is gone from java.net, so a program's java.net never reaches the network doors;
# they stay the defense for every other path (web/runner/doors.js).
#
# Written from TeaVM's own sources (classlib/src/main/java/org/teavm/classlib/java/net/) and the pinned
# JDK's observed output; no OpenJDK source was opened.
#
# Applies to: github.com/konsoletyper/teavm tag 0.13.1 (b3a245b7)

